/-- **Theorem A `triangles_in_the_plane`** (Geometric conclusion). Let $\Delta\subset\R^2$
consist of three noncollinear points. If $E\subset\R^2$ is measurable and $d^*(E)>0$, there is
$\lambda_0>0$ such that, for every $\lambda\ge\lambda_0$, the set $E$ contains a translate of a
rotation of $\lambda\Delta$.

Here $\Delta = \{a, b, c\}$ and $d^*$ is `upperBanachDensity`. -/
theorem triangles_in_the_plane (a b c : ℝ²) (hΔ : ¬ Collinear ℝ ({a, b, c} : Set ℝ²)) (E : Set ℝ²)
    (hE : NullMeasurableSet E volume) (hd : 0 < upperBanachDensity E) :
    ∃ lam₀ : ℝ, 0 < lam₀ ∧ ∀ lam : ℝ, lam₀ ≤ lam →
      ∃ (x : ℝ²) (t : ℝ), ∀ p ∈ ({a, b, c} : Set ℝ²), x + rot t (lam • p) ∈ E :=
  den_main_general a b c hΔ E hE hd

/-- **Theorem B `smoothing`** (Uniform smoothing). Let $J(x_1,x_2)=(-x_2,x_1)$ and
$M=\alpha I+\beta J$ with $\beta\ne0$, let $\sigma$ be uniform probability measure on the unit
circle, and for a finite measure $\nu$ let
$$\Lambda_\nu(f_0,f_1,f_2)=\iint f_0(x)f_1(x+u)f_2(x+Mu)\,d\nu(u)\,dx,$$
$\nu_s$ the pushforward of $\nu$ under $u\mapsto su$, and $\|\Lambda_\nu\|$ the
$L^2\times L^2\times L^\infty$ operator norm. For every nonnegative $\rho\in L^1(\R^2)$ with
$\int\rho=1$, put $\rho_\varepsilon(u)=\varepsilon^{-2}\rho(u/\varepsilon)$. Then
$$\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\sup_{s>0}
\big\|\Lambda_{(\sigma*\rho_\varepsilon-\sigma)_s}\big\|=0.$$
The same statement holds with the $L^\infty$ slot at any of the three vertices (here `i`).

The norm is `endNorm`. -/
theorem smoothing (α β : ℝ) (hβ : β ≠ 0) (ρ : ℝ² → ℝ) (hρ0 : ∀ x, 0 ≤ ρ x)
